leader5_type       = [[Deputy Speaker of the House of Representatives of Nigeria|Deputy Speaker of the House of Representatives]]\n| leader5            = [[Benjamin Kalu]]\n| party5             = ([[All Progressives Congress|APC]])\n| election5          = 13 June 2023\n| leader6_type       = House Majority Leader\n| leader6            = [[Julius Ihonvbere]]\n| party6             = ([[All Progressives Congress|APC]])\n| election6          = 4 July 2023\n| members            = '''469'''<br/>109 Senators<br/>{{nowrap|360 Representatives}}\n| house1             = [[Senate of Nigeria|Senate]]\n| structure1         = Nigeria 10th Senate.svg\n| structure1_res     = 250px\n| structure1_alt     = Current Structure of the Nigerian Senate\n| political_groups1  = {{legend|#87BEEB|[[All Progressives Congress]]: 75 seats}} {{legend|#006903|[[Peoples Democratic Party (Nigeria)|Peoples Democratic Party]]: 26 seats}} {{legend|#F0DC82|[[Labour Party (Nigeria)|Labour Party]]: 4 seats}} {{legend|#00923F|[[All Progressives Grand Alliance]]: 2 seats}} {{legend|#00FF00|[[New Nigeria People's Party]]: 1 seat}} {{legend|#E67818|[[Social Democratic Party (Nigeria)|Social Democratic Party]]: 1 seat}}\n| house2             = [[House of Representatives (Nigeria)|House of Representatives]]\n| structure2         = Nigeria_10thHouseofReps.svg\n| structure2_res     = 250px\n| structure2_alt     = \n| political_groups2  = {{legend|#87BEEB|[[All Progressives Congress]]: 242 seats}} {{nowrap|{{legend|#006903|[[Peoples Democratic Party (Nigeria)|People's Democratic Party]]: 72 seats}}}} {{nowrap|{{legend|#F0DC82|[[Labour Party (Nigeria)|Labour Party]]: 22 seats}}}} {{nowrap|{{legend|#00FF00|[[New Nigeria People's Party]]: 15 seats}}}} {{nowrap|{{legend|#00923F |[[All Progressives Grand Alliance]]: 5 seats}}}} {{nowrap|{{legend|#e67819|[[Social Democratic Party (Nigeria)|Social Democratic Party]]: 2 seats}}}} {{nowrap|{{legend|#00A86B |[[African Democratic Congress]]: 1 seats}}}} {{nowrap|{{legend|#810000|[[Young Progressives Party]]: 1 seat}}}}\n\n| voting_system1     = [[First-past-the-post voting|First-past-the-post]]\n| voting_system2     = [[First-past-the-post voting|First-past-the-post]]\n| last_election1     = [[2023 Nigerian Senate election|25 February 2023]]\n| last_election2     = [[2023 Nigerian House of Representatives election|25 February 2023]]\n| next_election1     = 27 February 2027\n| next_election2     = 27 February 2027\n| session_room       = National_Assembly_of_Nigeria.jpg\n\n| meeting_place      = [[National Assembly Complex]]<br>[[Abuja, FCT|Abuja]]<br>[[Federal Republic of Nigeria]]\n| website            = {{URL|http://www.nass.gov.ng/}}\n}}\n\nThe '''National Assembly of the Federal Republic of Nigeria''' is a [[bicameralism|bicameral]] [[legislature]] established under section 4 of the [[Constitution of Nigeria]].{{sfn|Usonka|2019|p=89}}{{efn|The section 4 of the Nigerian Constitution states that \"The legislative power of the Federal Republic of Nigeria shall be vested in the National Assembly for the federal which shall consist of a Senate and House of Representatives.{{sfn|Usonka|2019|p=89}}}} The body consists of 109 members of the [[Senate of Nigeria|Senate]] and 360 members from the [[House of Representatives (Nigeria)|House of Representatives]];<ref>{{Cite news | last=Eribake | first=Akintayo | title=Law and leadership in the 21st century Nigeria (4) |newspaper=[[Vanguard (Nigeria)|Vanguard]]| date=4 May 2011 | url=https://www.vanguardngr.com/2011/05/law-and-leadership-in-the-21st-century-nigeria-4/|location=Lagos, Nigeria| access-date=9 February 2025}}</ref> There are three senators from each of the [[States of Nigeria]] and one senator representing the [[Federal Capital Territory (Nigeria)|Federal Capital Territory]] and [[single-member district]], [[plurality voting]] in the House of Representatives.\n\n==Leadership==\n{{Further|President of the Senate of Nigeria|Speaker of the House of Representatives of Nigeria}}\nSection 50 of the Nigerian constitution creates the [[President of the Senate of Nigeria]] and the [[Speaker of the House of Representatives of Nigeria]], as heads of their respective chambers. Section 53 states: \"At any joint sitting of the Senate and House of Representatives, the President of the Senate shall preside, and in his absence, the Speaker of the House of Representatives shall preside.\"<ref>{{Cite news | title= Explainer: Is House of Reps Speaker equal to Senate President?|newspaper=[[Premium Times]]| url=https://www.premiumtimesng.com/features-and-interviews/748636-explainer-is-house-of-reps-speaker-equal-to-senate-president.html | access-date=9 February 2025}}</ref> The incumbent President of the Senate is [[Godswill Akpabio]] and the Speaker of the House is [[Tajudeen Abbas]]. Both assumed office on 13 June 2023.<ref>{{cite web | last=Asadu | first=Chinedu | title=Legislators from Nigeria's ruling party elected as Senate president, House speaker | website=AP News | date=13 June 2023 | url=https://apnews.com/article/nigeria-akpabio-senate-house-parliament-tinubu-0a2a1653462b7796a9210c9627019027 | access-date=9 February 2025}}</ref>\n\n==Functions==\nThe 1999 Constitution of Nigeria conferred on the National Assembly, the power over Nigeria's [[National Treasury]] and issues involving the prosecution of war with another country. The body also regulates how the [[President of Nigeria|President]] functions.{{sfn|Arowolo|2010|p=27}} Since the restoration of [[democracy|democratic rule]] in 1999, the Assembly has been said to be in a \"learning process\" that has witnessed the election and removal of several presidents of the Senate, allegations of [[political corruption|corruption]], slow passage of private member's bills and the creation of ineffective committees to satisfy numerous interests.\n\nIn spite of a more than two-thirds majority control of the Assembly by the then ruling [[People's Democratic Party (Nigeria)|Peoples Democratic Party]] (PDP), the PDP government led by [[Goodluck Jonathan]] and the Assembly have been known more for their disagreements than for their cooperation. Jonathan has been accused of being slow to implement policy. Many bills, some from as long ago as 2007, are still{{when|date=August 2023}} awaiting the president's assent. While the Assembly has made strong and often popular efforts to assert its authority and independence against the [[executive branch|executive]], it is still viewed generally in a negative light by the media and many of the Nigerian people.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Odeyemi |first=Temitayo |date=11 October 2023 |title=Nigerian legislatures need to repair their poor relationship with the people |url=https://blogs.lse.ac.uk/africaatlse/2023/10/11/nigerian-legislatures-need-to-repair-their-poor-relationship-with-the-people/ |access-date=11 October 2023 |website=Africa at LSE blog}}</ref> The Assembly sits for a period of at most four years, after which time the president is required to dissolve it and call a new Assembly into session.\n\nThe Senate has the unique power to impeach [[judge]]s and other high officials of the executive, including the federal [[Auditor|auditor-general]] and the members of the electoral and revenue commissions. This power is, however, subject to a prior request by the president. The Senate also confirms the president's nomination of senior [[diplomats]], members of the federal [[Cabinet (government)|cabinet]], federal [[Judiciary|judicial]] appointments, and independent federal commissions.\n\nBefore any bill may become law, it must be agreed to by both the House and the Senate, and receive the president's assent. Should the president delay or refuse assent ([[veto]]) the bill, the Assembly may pass the law by two-thirds of both chambers and overrule the veto and the president's consent will not be required.\n\nThe [[National Institute for Legislative Studies]] (NILS)<ref>{{cite web|url=http://nils.gov.ng/about.php|title=National Institute for Legislative Studies|work=NILS }}</ref> is an organ of the National Assembly established by an [[Act of Parliament]]. Former President Jonathan signed into law the National Institute for Legislative Studies Act 2011 on 2 March 2011 following the passage of the same by the Senate and the House of Representatives.  NILS builds on the successes of the Policy Analysis and Research Project, established in 2003 as a capacity building institution of the National Assembly with the financial support of the African Capacity Building Foundation. NILS has as its core objectives to provide quality academic and professional research, policy analysis, training, documentation and advocacy on democratic governance and legislative practice and procedures.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nassnig.org/nass2/acts.php|title=National Assembly - Federal Republic of Nigeria|work=National Assembly}}</ref> The functions of NILS are similar to the services offered to the [[US Congress]] by [[Congressional Research Service]], [[Congressional Budget Office]], and [[Library of Congress]], only on a lesser scale as the institute was just established.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.thenationonlineng.net/2011/index.php/mobile/news/47426-constitution-amendment:-%E2%80%98state-assemblies%E2%80%99ve-chance-for-autonomy%E2%80%99.html|title=The Nation Newspaper Nigeria - Read Latest Nigeria News|newspaper=[[The Nation (Nigeria)|The Nation]]|location=Nigeria}}</ref> The institute has been renamed the National Institute for Legislative and Democratic Studies, following the amendment to the act establishing it.\n\n==State delegates==\n{{colbegin}}\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Abia|Abia]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Adamawa|Adamawa]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Akwa Ibom|Akwa Ibom]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Anambra|Anambra]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Bauchi|Bauchi]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Bayelsa|Bayelsa]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Benue|Benue]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Borno|Borno]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Cross River|Cross River]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Delta|Delta]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Ebonyi|Ebonyi]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Edo|Edo]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Ekiti|Ekiti]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Enugu|Enugu]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Gombe|Gombe]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Imo|Imo]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Jigawa|Jigawa]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Kaduna|Kaduna]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Kano|Kano]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Katsina|Katsina]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Kebbi|Kebbi]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Kogi|Kogi]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Kwara|Kwara]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Lagos|Lagos]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Nasarawa|Nasarawa]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Niger|Niger]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Ogun|Ogun]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Ondo|Ondo]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Osun|Osun]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Oyo|Oyo]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Plateau|Plateau]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Rivers|Rivers]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Sokoto|Sokoto]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Taraba|Taraba]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Yobe|Yobe]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from Zamfara|Zamfara]]\n#[[Nigerian National Assembly delegation from the Federal Capital Territory|FCT]]\n{{colend}}\n\n== Women in the National Assembly ==\nCurrently, there are a total of 21 women serving in the National Assembly, 8 in the Senate<ref name=\"data.ipu.org\">{{Cite web |title=Data on women in national parliament |url=https://data.ipu.org/node/125/data-on-women |access-date=2022-05-12 |website=Parline: the IPU’s Open Data Platform |language=en}}</ref> and 13 in the House of Representatives.<ref name=\"data.ipu.org\"/> In total, women make up around 4.47% percent of the National Assembly. The first and only female to serve as the speaker of the [[House of Representatives (Nigeria)|Nigerian House of Representatives]] was [[Patricia Etteh]] who served a four-month term from June to October 2007.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2007-10-12 |title=House of Representatives Member {{!}} Honourable Patricia Etteh |url=http://nassnig.org/House/Personaldata/Osun/etteh.htm |access-date=2022-05-12 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071012134925/http://nassnig.org/House/Personaldata/Osun/etteh.htm |archive-date=12 October 2007 }}</ref> The only female to have held the position of governor in Nigerian history was [[Virginia Ngozi Etiaba|Dame Virginia Ngozi Etiaba]] who held the office of [[governor of Anambra State]] for three months after the previous governor was impeached for alleged gross misconduct.{{citation needed|date=February 2025}}\n\nIn Nigeria, men and women have an equal right to participate in public office, which is guaranteed to them by the constitution. Section 40 of the [[Constitution of Nigeria|1999 Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria]] bestows upon Nigerian citizens the right to belong to any political party, trade union or any other association for the protection of his interests. Many initiatives have been taken by the Nigerian government to promote the participation of women in politics and public service. In the 2000 National Policy on Women, the Nigerian government pledged to ensure that women participate in politics equally to men, implementing an [[affirmative action]] quota of 30% increase in participation. In the 2003 elections, some parties waived nomination fees for women in order to lower the barriers to participation.{{citation needed|date=February 2025}}  There are many civil society groups that focus on the interests of women who want to participate in politics. The Forum of Nigerian Women in Politics is an influential group that operates with the goal of empowering women. They have requested that the Nigerian government yield 30% female representation in government appointments.<ref>{{Cite journal |url=http://www.ajol.info/index.php/ajpssi/article/view/48152 |title=Gender, Power And Political Leadership in Nigeria: Problems And Prospects |journal=African Journal for the Psychological Study of Social Issues |date=2009 |volume=12 |issue=1–2 |publisher=Department of Psychology, University of Ibadan |language=English |oclc=711054749|last1=Agbalajobi |first1=D. T. }}</ref>\n\nWomen in Nigeria face many barriers to political participation. These include cultural practices, threat of intimidation or violence, high cost of election, inadequacy of willing and educated women, and issues regarding indigeneity. Women who are married outside their constituencies of birth but run for election in the constituencies of their marriage are often marked as non-indigene by the community.{{citation needed|date=February 2025}}\n\nWomen face multiple obstacles that limit them in their political participation, and this has an effect on the number of women who are represented in government. These obstacles are related to the high cost of politics, which prevents women for standing for positions, as some women are not able to afford the mandatory expression of interest and nomination forms. These forms are required by political parties to run for positions on their platforms in addition to the campaign costs. Women are disproportionately affected by this because there is inadequate [[Female education|access to education for women]] compared to their male counterparts and poor access to education translates to poor access to gainful employment, unpaid labor, unequal access to inheritance rights, and discrimination - all obstacles that limit the participation of women in politics.<ref name=\":1\">{{Cite web |last=Agbalajobi |first=Damilola |title=Nigeria has few women in politics: here's why, and what to do about it |url=http://theconversation.com/nigeria-has-few-women-in-politics-heres-why-and-what-to-do-about-it-159578 |access-date=2022-05-12 |website=The Conversation |date=3 May 2021 |language=en}}</ref>\n\nMercy Ette argues that there has been less participation of women in politics as a result of the influence of the media. She argues that the media has reinforced a [[patriarchy]] in politics and has downplayed the participation of women in politics.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ette |first=Mercy |date=2017-10-03 |title=Where are the women? Evaluating visibility of Nigerian female politicians in news media space |url=https://doi.org/10.1080/0966369X.2017.1387104 |journal=Gender, Place & Culture |volume=24 |issue=10 |pages=1480–1497 |doi=10.1080/0966369X.2017.1387104 |s2cid=149339769 |issn=0966-369X}}</ref> The failure of political parties to choose women as strong candidates has also contributed greatly to lack of effective participation of women in politics. These women are often given subordinate positions where they are able to change little to nothing or affect decisions in the political society.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Okoosi |first=Simbine Antonia Taiye |date=2012-05-01 |title=Gender politics and the 2011 elections |url=https://journals.co.za/doi/abs/10.10520/EJC123796 |journal=Journal of African Elections |volume=11 |issue=1 |pages=74–99 |doi=10.20940/JAE/2012/v11i1a5 |doi-broken-date=1 July 2025 |hdl=10520/EJC123796 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Despite the challenges faced by women in Nigerian politics, there has been an increasing number of female participation in politics over the years. In 1999 3 women were elected to the senate and 12 to the House of Representatives after two rounds of elections. Later the number of women in senate has gone up to 8 in 2007, but this number later dropped in 2011.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ette |first1=Mercy |last2=Akpan-Obong |first2=Patience |date=2022-03-13 |title=Negotiating Access and Privilege: Politics of Female Participation and Representation in Nigeria |journal=Journal of Asian and African Studies |volume=58 |issue=7 |language=en |pages=1291–1306 |doi=10.1177/00219096221084253 |s2cid=247456340 |issn=0021-9096|doi-access=free }}</ref> The overall national average for women participation is around 6% for elective and appointive positions which is below the West African sub regional average of 15%. Nigeria ranks the 32nd out of the 35 sub Saharan countries when it comes to representation of women in politics. The 2023 elections faced similar criticisms, as there were only 6 female aspirants who joined the [[President of Nigeria|presidential race]] to succeed [[Muhammadu Buhari]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2022-04-16 |title=2023 PRESIDENCY: Where are the women? |url=https://www.vanguardngr.com/2022/04/2023-presidency-where-are-the-women/ |location=Lagos, Nigeria|access-date=2022-05-12 |newspaper=[[Vanguard (Nigeria)|Vanguard]]|language=en-GB}}</ref>\n\n==Notes==\n{{notelist}}\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n==Sources==\n{{Refbegin}}\n* {{cite journal | last=Usonka| first=Cornelia Orieoma| journal=African Journal of Politics and Administrative Studies|volume=12|issue=1|date=2019 |title=Parliamentary Resolutions and Good Governance in Nigeria: A Study of 7th Senate of the National Assembly|url=https://www.ajol.info/index.php/ajpas/article/view/247157/233778 | access-date=9 February 2025}}\n* {{cite web | title= Oversight functions of the legislature: An instrument for nation building|url=https://www.ajol.info/index.php/naujilj/article/view/138178/127909 | first=GA |last=Arowolo |date=2010 | access-date=9 February 2025|work=ajol.info}}\n* {{cite book 